<p>After hail, wind, fire, or water damages a San Marcos home or business, the repair has to meet the building codes in force today, not the ones in place when the building went up. That gap between “how it was built” and “how it must be rebuilt” causes a lot of confusion on insurance claims. This guide explains the San Marcos building codes that apply to insurance repairs, when you need a permit, who to call, and how ordinance or law coverage fits in. It is based on information published by the City of San Marcos and other sources cited below.</p>
<div class="callout"><span class="callout-title">Verified October 20, 2026</span><p>We checked the City of San Marcos sources linked on this page on October 20, 2026. Codes, fees, and procedures change. This page is general information, not code consulting or legal advice. Confirm current requirements with the San Marcos Permit Center, or with your own city or county if the property is outside San Marcos city limits, before starting work.</p></div>

<h2>San Marcos building codes at a glance</h2>
<p>The City of San Marcos adopted the 2021 editions of the International Code Council’s model codes, plus the 2020 National Electrical Code, through <strong>Ordinance No. 2023-09</strong> (second reading February 7, 2023). The city’s <a href="https://www.sanmarcostx.gov/1289/Codes-Ordinances" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Codes &amp; Ordinances page</a> states that the codes have been <strong>in effect since April 1, 2023</strong>. Several codes carry local amendments, so the city’s version can differ from the model text you might find at <a href="https://www.iccsafe.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">iccsafe.org</a>.</p>

