<h2>Roof repair and replacement in San Marcos</h2>
<p>Roofs are where codes and claims meet most often here. Hail is the most frequently reported severe weather in the Hays County NOAA record, with 180 reports, and the largest stone on record, 4.5 inches, fell just north of San Marcos on March 16, 2000. Composition shingles cover most homes in the city’s subdivisions, while standing-seam and exposed-fastener metal roofs are common on Hill Country homes, barns, and outbuildings, and flat or low-slope roofs are typical on commercial buildings along I-35.</p>
<p>The local reroof rule is good news for most homeowners: replacing a hail-damaged shingle roof with new shingles, or a metal roof with similar metal, typically does not require a city permit. Switching to a heavier covering, for example from composition shingles to concrete tile, is the situation the amendment carves out, and that also raises questions about whether the existing framing can carry the load. If the roof deck or framing needs structural repair, plan on talking with the Permit Center.</p>
<p>No permit does not mean no rules. The model IRC states that an exemption from permit requirements does not authorize work that violates the code, so the new roof should still meet the adopted code and the manufacturer’s installation instructions for underlayment, flashing, drip edge, fastening, and ventilation. Insurer estimates sometimes price the shingles or panels but leave out these components. Whether they are owed depends on whether they are part of repairing covered damage and on any ordinance or law coverage in your policy. For more, see <a href="/services/roof-damage-insurance-claims/">roof damage insurance claims</a> and <a href="/how-to-review-underpaid-roof-insurance-estimate/">how to review an underpaid roof insurance estimate</a>.</p>

<h2>Wind-resistant construction</h2>
<p>San Marcos has a long record of damaging straight-line wind. NOAA describes 70 to 80 mph winds along I-35 on January 13, 2007 that tore metal roofs off several buildings, and a 75 mph gust on June 9, 2019 that <a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/eventdetails.jsp?id=835457" target="_blank" rel="noopener">partly peeled the metal roof off a house in San Marcos</a>. Wind requirements come from the adopted IRC and IBC and the engineering standards they reference, which set design wind speeds by location and building type. We do not list a design wind speed here; your contractor, designer, or the Permit Center can confirm it for a specific project.</p>
<p>A common point of confusion: the Texas Department of Insurance windstorm certification program (WPI-8) applies only inside the <a href="https://www.twia.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Texas Windstorm Insurance Association</a> catastrophe area on the coast. <strong>Hays County is not in that area</strong>, so San Marcos properties usually get wind and hail coverage from their regular insurer (or the Texas FAIR Plan where applicable), and WPI-8 certification does not apply.</p>

