<h2>Structural, electrical, and plumbing repairs</h2>
<p>Fire, fallen trees, and long-running water leaks can reach framing, trusses, wiring, plumbing, and foundations. That work generally needs permits and inspections, may need an engineer, and must meet the 2021 codes and the 2020 NEC. Because the International Existing Building Code is part of the adopted package, how much upgrading a repair triggers can depend on the scope and type of work. Examples of items that can come into play include:</p>
<ul>
<li>Smoke and carbon monoxide alarm requirements tied to permitted interior work.</li>
<li>Electrical updates when walls are opened and circuits are replaced after a fire or flood.</li>
<li>Insulation and air-sealing requirements under the 2021 IECC when those components are replaced.</li>
<li>Engineer-designed foundation repairs under the city’s foundation amendment.</li>
</ul>
<p>Ask the Permit Center or a licensed contractor which upgrades your project actually requires, and get that in writing where you can. If your home is one of the older houses in the historic neighborhoods near downtown, ask the city whether any additional review applies before replacing windows, siding, or roofing.</p>

<h2>Inspections and contractor responsibilities</h2>
<p>Permitted work is inspected in stages and finishes with a final inspection. Inspection results and correction notices make strong claim documents, because they show what the city actually required on the job. If an inspector calls for an item the insurer’s estimate left out, that notice supports a <a href="/services/insurance-claim-supplements/">supplement</a>.</p>
<p>On most jobs the contractor pulls any required permit, builds to code, and schedules inspections. Before work starts, ask the contractor to confirm whether a permit is needed, explain which code items are in the price, and list code-required work separately from like-kind repairs. That makes it far easier to present code items to the insurer. Keep in mind that under Texas Insurance Code Chapter 4102 a public adjuster may not take part in repairing a property on a claim they adjust, so your contractor and your public adjuster have separate roles.</p>

<h2>Ordinance or law coverage</h2>
<p>Ordinance or law coverage pays, up to its limit, for the increased cost of meeting building codes when repairing covered damage. It is often a separate coverage or endorsement, and the limit may be a percentage of the dwelling coverage or a fixed dollar amount. Some policies carry little or none. Some pay the code portion only after the work is completed. Read your declarations page and policy forms, and see our article on <a href="/building-codes-insurance-repairs-san-marcos/">building codes and insurance repairs in San Marcos</a> for a deeper explanation.</p>
<div class="callout callout-warn"><span class="callout-title">Code compliance is not the same as coverage</span><p>A requirement under the San Marcos building codes does not automatically mean your insurer must pay for it. Payment for code upgrades depends on your policy’s ordinance or law coverage, its limit, and its conditions.</p></div>

<h2>Nearby cities and unincorporated Hays County</h2>
<p>The City of San Marcos codes apply only inside city limits and to qualifying ETJ projects. Neighboring cities run their own permit offices, adopt their own code editions and amendments, and set their own fees and inspection procedures. That includes <a href="/service-areas/kyle/">Kyle</a>, <a href="/service-areas/buda/">Buda</a>, <a href="/service-areas/wimberley/">Wimberley</a>, and <a href="/service-areas/dripping-springs/">Dripping Springs</a> in Hays County; <a href="/service-areas/lockhart/">Lockhart</a> and <a href="/service-areas/martindale/">Martindale</a> in Caldwell County; <a href="/service-areas/new-braunfels/">New Braunfels</a> in Comal and Guadalupe counties; and <a href="/service-areas/seguin/">Seguin</a> in Guadalupe County. Contact the city where the property is located before work begins.</p>
<p>In <strong>unincorporated Hays County</strong>, the county does not issue residential building permits or enforce a residential building code. It does handle development, septic (OSSF), and floodplain permitting, and it applies the 2018 International Fire Code to new commercial and multifamily buildings. Details are on the county’s <a href="https://www.hayscountytx.gov/595/Development-Permits" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Development Permits page</a>. For a repair in an unincorporated area, ask your contractor which standards and manufacturer instructions the work will follow, and get that scope in writing.</p>

<h2>When code items lead to a claim dispute</h2>
<p>Disagreements usually involve whether a code item was required, whether it relates to covered damage, or whether the policy has enough ordinance or law coverage. Useful steps:</p>
<ul>
<li>Get written confirmation of the requirement from the Permit Center, an inspector, or a licensed professional.</li>
<li>Have your contractor itemize code-required work separately.</li>
<li>Compare the insurer’s estimate line by line with the actual scope, or request an <a href="/services/insurance-estimate-review/">insurance estimate review</a>.</li>
<li>If the disagreement is over the amount of loss, your policy’s <a href="/services/insurance-appraisal/">appraisal clause</a> may apply. For coverage disputes or legal deadlines, consult a Texas attorney.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Confirm before you build</h2>
<p>This page reflects our review of City of San Marcos sources as of October 20, 2026. Before starting any repair, contact the Permit Center at 630 E. Hopkins Street, 512-805-2630, or through the <a href="https://www.sanmarcostx.gov/1000/Permit-Center" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Permit Center web page</a> to confirm permit, code, and inspection requirements. If you have questions about how those requirements affect your insurance claim, a <a href="/">San Marcos public adjuster</a> can help through a <a href="/free-claim-review/">Free Claim Review</a>.</p>
